STYLE: A grand hand-made toy theatre production built from painted card. Every piece is a flat cutout of thick card about 4 mm thick, cut by hand with a craft knife. The front of each piece is painted in the manner of a Russian Palekh lacquer miniature: slender, graceful figures in fine egg tempera, deep glossy black lacquer showing between and around the shapes, fine gold-leaf hatching on the folds of the clothes, on the snow and on the ice, small precise brushwork, jewel-bright colour against the black. Bold, clear silhouettes that read from the back row. Puppet joints are brass split pins. Palette: lacquer black #0E0C0B, gold leaf #C9A04A, ice white #E9ECEE, frost blue #7FA3BF, deep night blue #1F3550, birch white #EDE6D6, ochre #B8862E, faded crimson #9A3A34 used only in small touches of clothing, warm lamp gold #F2B85A used only for flames and lit windows, with raw card brown #A57E52 at cut edges. The mood is hunger, deep cold, endurance and a brother's love for his little sister.
CONSTRAINTS: No text, no letters, no Cyrillic, no numbers, no signature, no watermark. No five-pointed stars, no red stars, no hammer and sickle, no flags, no banners, no insignia, no badges, no medals, no portraits of leaders, no church domes, no crosses, no icons, no religious figures, no weapons, no tanks, no aircraft. No photographic people, no 3D render look, no plastic, no anime style, no cartoon style.
REFERENCES: Image 1 is the look reference. Match its Palekh lacquer miniature painting, black lacquer ground, gold hatching, palette, raw card edges and figure style. Do not copy its composition.
SUBJECT: The four pieces of a marionette of Masha as a grown woman of thirty in the 1960s, facing three-quarters to the left: (1) the head with fair hair under a plain grey headscarf, a calm grave face, and the bare neck; (2) the body from the neck base down to plain boots, in a long grey wool coat buttoned down the front, standing, with NO arms; (3) her RIGHT arm from shoulder to an open bare hand, grey coat sleeve; (4) her LEFT arm from shoulder to an open bare hand, grey coat sleeve.
COMPOSITION: A flat lay of the separate cardboard pieces of ONE jointed puppet, all at the same scale, seen straight from the front on a transparent background. Each piece is a separate cutout with clear empty space around it. No piece touches or overlaps another piece. No shadow, no rod, no strings, no stand, no stage.
JOINTS: The body has three clearly visible round brass split pins: one at the centre of the base of the neck and one on each shoulder. The body ends at the neck base with no head and has no arms. The head piece ends in a bare neck with no collar, with one brass pin at the bottom of the neck. Each arm piece has one brass pin at the very top of the shoulder. One arm is a RIGHT arm and the other is a LEFT arm, each hanging straight down from its shoulder pin, with the thumbs on the correct sides. The head is drawn at a natural size for the body.
